# FOUNDATION 2/4 — COMPOSE RECOMPOSER WORK-WAKEUP / SNAPSHOT INVALIDATION (OPEN HIGH-FAN-OUT FRONTIER)

This second foundation section is separate from #364's first section (classic View.invalidate → ViewRoot traversal). It targets a Compose-specific base contract that can prevent modern Compose APKs from producing any app-owned pixels even after ComposeView host setup, measure/layout, and the first draw walk are present.

## 0. Local evidence — this is not a speculative “fix Compose” task

Re-read these actual repository records before editing:
- **#376 / UPP-005** in `docs/UPSTREAM_REPLACEMENT_PLAN.jsonl`: still recorded as `PENDING_RESEARCH`; four distinct Compose fronts were already separated: ComposeView missing from the class index (BlockBlast), host exists but no app draw ops (Dooz), R8-renamed internals / missing class identity (Dooz), and rendering backend not yet observable. Do not merge these into one root and do not start a large Compose port.
- **#383 CONT-23/CONT-25**: CONT-23 identified F-277 scheduler starvation; the F-277 poll-timeout adjustment let the stranded 16 ms work run, but did not make composition progress. CONT-25 says the Recomposer runner's wake-up work does not materialize as queue work; only 13 trampoline tasks appeared in the whole 15-second run. It identifies the next frontier as invalidation delivery: produceState / LaunchedEffect collection dispatch / StateFlow first emit / GlobalSnapshotManager notification does not become runner work. That report explicitly does NOT claim real app-owned pixels were fixed.
- **#350 is already closed for the LocalDensity/WindowRecomposer-host symptom**: at the reported S106 HEAD, that exact ISE occurred zero times in three fresh runs; the code reached `Recomposer.composeInitial$runtime → processCompositionError`. Do not reopen LocalDensity/decor-root work merely because Compose content is still absent. The remaining composition/invalidation failure is a different frontier.
- Before action, re-fetch the current HEAD and UPP-005/registry because these records are historical snapshots, not an assertion of current HEAD status.

## 1. The answer to the Recomposer wake-up puzzle ALREADY EXISTS

### Direct Q&A answer
Stack Overflow question “When will awaitWorkAvailable resume from runRecomposeAndApplyChanges in Recomposer?”:
https://stackoverflow.com/questions/72778714/when-will-awaitworkavailable-resume-from-runrecomposeandapplychanges-in-recompos

The accepted answer explains the exact mechanism: `deriveStateLocked()` returns the stored `workContinuation` when state transitions to `PendingWork`; caller paths then resume that returned continuation after leaving the lock. It gives examples from:
- `Snapshot.registerApplyObserver`: snapshot changes are collected, state derived, returned continuation resumed;
- `invalidate(composition)`: add composition to `compositionInvalidations`, derive state, resume returned continuation;
- `invalidateScope(scope)`: add snapshot invalidation, derive state, resume returned continuation.

This is a strikingly close match to the local unresolved symptom (“Recomposer runner never wakes / invalidation work never reaches its queue”). It is a **high-priority same-mechanism hypothesis**, but not proof of local identity until MiniAndroid traces the analogous state transition.

### Authoritative AndroidX source, verify the same contract directly
Current Recomposer source:
https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/support/+/refs/heads/androidx-main/compose/runtime/runtime/src/commonMain/kotlin/androidx/compose/runtime/Recomposer.kt

Useful exact symbols/lines in the current fetched source (line numbers can move; use symbol anchors):
- `workContinuation` field (near line 251);
- `deriveStateLocked()` (near 324): chooses `PendingWork` when snapshot/composition invalidations, apply work, movable content or frame-clock awaiters exist; when becoming PendingWork it returns `workContinuation.also { workContinuation = null }` (roughly 349–362);
- `awaitWorkAvailable()` (near 941–965): checks `hasSchedulingWork`, then installs `workContinuation = co` only if no work appeared during the lock check, otherwise resumes immediately after the check;
- `Snapshot.registerApplyObserver` in `recompositionRunner` (near 980–999): records changed state values, invokes `deriveStateLocked()`, and resumes the returned continuation outside the lock;
- `invalidate(composition)` and `invalidateScope(scope)` (near 1463–1478): mutate invalidation sets, derive state, resume outside the synchronized block;
- `runRecomposeAndApplyChanges` (near 550 onward): waits for work, drains modification records, aligns frame work with the frame clock, sends frames, delivers snapshot apply notifications, recomposes, then applies changes.

The critical law is NOT “wake the runner by posting an arbitrary frame” and NOT “keep polling continuously.” It is: **state transitions that create pending work must hand the parked continuation out of the lock and resume it; the waiter must not lose work racing with suspension.** The AndroidX implementation deliberately checks for work both before and under the lock to avoid a lost-wakeup race.

API semantics also matter: Snapshot.apply notifications are deferred for state objects changed outside a snapshot until `Snapshot.sendApplyNotifications()` is called. Reference:
https://developer.android.com/reference/kotlin/androidx/compose/runtime/snapshots/Snapshot#sendApplyNotifications()

## 2. MiniAndroid causal hypothesis — test exact mechanism, not a guessed fix

Potential divergence points, in order:
A. state update/flow collector never executes because its coroutine/dispatcher task was not launched or enqueued;
B. Compose MutableState write occurs but no apply notification is delivered;
C. GlobalSnapshotManager/observer is not registered or not reached;
D. snapshot observer runs but changed object is filtered as not composition-read;
E. invalidation is added to a set but does not transition the Recomposer state to PendingWork;
F. pending work is recognized but parked continuation is not captured/resumed, or it resumes while the frame-pump has already declared quiescence;
G. continuation resumes but dispatch/trampoline work is lost;
H. runner wakes and recomposes but the composition errors (inspect `processCompositionError` and the original exception separately);
I. composition applies changes, but the View/Canvas render path fails later.

Only the first measured divergence is eligible for a fix. Do not assume F is the local root just because the SO answer describes the exact upstream mechanism.

## 3. Required diagnostic points (bounded, env-gated, no permanent log flood)

Add temporary diagnostics at the existing local Compose scheduler/dispatcher boundary, not inside arbitrary app packages:
- Recomposer/runner state at Idle → PendingWork and PendingWork → Idle transitions;
- count/identity of the parked work continuation (created, taken, resumed, cancelled; avoid exposing object pointer as semantic identity);
- source event: direct composition invalidation, scope invalidation, snapshot apply notification, frame-clock awaiter or movable-content insert;
- whether `Snapshot.sendApplyNotifications` runs after a state write and whether its observer callback executes;
- dispatcher queue/trampoline enqueue → dequeue → execute IDs, with timestamps/virtual time and parent-task ID;
- runner wake → `awaitWorkAvailable` return → `recordComposerModifications` → `withFrameNanos` → `performRecompose` → `applyChanges`;
- the first thrown Throwable, exact method/pc and handler delivery; never swallow an original composition error into a downstream generic “no pixels”.

Every event must carry a monotonically increasing causal sequence so the log can prove whether work was lost, filtered, delayed, or only not yet due. Do not use wall-clock waiting as a workaround.

## 4. Minimal synthetic proof matrix

Build a tiny real Compose APK/DEX fixture (and use the version-pinned real Compose source already present where possible). It should produce a unique colored box with a counter/state value. Add named tests:
1. **Initial composition**: setContent runs and applies an identifiable node/color.
2. **Direct invalidation**: invalidate a known composition; runner wakes once and applies expected content.
3. **Snapshot state update**: read a MutableState in composition, change it, call the appropriate snapshot apply path; the observer runs, runner wakes, UI state/pixels change.
4. **LaunchedEffect/produceState**: enqueue a first state emission through the actual dispatcher/continuation chain; no hand-written fake state update.
5. **Race-before-park**: add invalidation after `hasSchedulingWork`'s first check but before the waiter parks; test must not lose it.
6. **Race-after-park**: park runner, then invalidate; the stored continuation must be resumed and work must execute.
7. **Coalescing**: several invalidations before one frame can coalesce, but the final state/value must be applied.
8. **Negative control**: no state change/invalidation means the runner can idle; no infinite polling and no unconditional extra frames.
9. **Error control**: composable throws; preserve and report the original error and state, not a false “scheduler idle” result.
10. **Two independent executions**: the same fixture works across at least two independent dispatch scheduling shapes or another independent Compose APK.

For each test assert the chain explicitly:
state write or invalidate → observer/invalidation set → PendingWork transition → exact continuation resumed → queued callback executed → frame/recompose → apply changes → app-owned draw op → pixel delta. Capture a counter/text value as well as pixels, so a draw background alone cannot pass.

## 5. Real APK and regression gates

- Primary: Dooz only if its current exact APK/SHA remains available; first verify whether the current trace still exhibits F-277/F-277-follow-on.
- Independent control: a second x86/x86_64-compatible Compose APK that reaches ComposeView and initial composition; if missing, report artifact absence as BLOCKED rather than pretending a second-family proof.
- Use the seven historically VERIFIED anchors as regression controls, plus the current live positive View/Canvas anchors. Preserve before-fix data. Run three fresh runs after any change.
- Require first app-owned draw ops and pixels, screenshot SHA/metrics and state-change pixels. A Recomposer state log, one scheduled callback, a frame, or a non-white window background is not sufficient.
- Re-run the canonical battery and golden checks; report exact current HEAD, binary SHA, commands, exit code and regression deltas.

## 6. Minimal likely implementation direction, only if the probe confirms it

Do not port Recomposer or Compose runtime wholesale. Implement/fix only the missing generic mechanism at the existing MiniAndroid coroutine/dispatcher + composition bridge boundary:
- a lossless pending-work state/continuation handoff;
- no lost wakeup between “check scheduling work” and “park continuation”;
- snapshot apply notifications forwarded into the recomposer's invalidation path when required;
- explicit enqueue/dequeue/execute contract for resumed continuations;
- frame-clock work only when a real frame awaiter/work item requires it, while preserving the existing deterministic scheduler.

Before patching, compare the local queue and state representation against the cited upstream implementation and the pinned Compose version used by the APK. If the local continuation/observer was not constructed at all, fix that earlier producer stage instead of patching the wake-up consumer.

## 7. Required results in THIS #364 comment thread

Return:
- current HEAD and the live status of UPP-005/F-277/any duplicate root IDs;
- exact local functions/files corresponding to `deriveStateLocked`, `awaitWorkAvailable`, observer registration, state write/apply, continuation resume and dispatcher enqueue/dequeue;
- a line-by-line mismatch table against the upstream symbols;
- named probe stage, positive/negative control outputs and first divergent causal sequence;
- whether the SO answer directly matched, only matched the semantic mechanism, or was rejected;
- actual two-target/3-run render evidence or honest BLOCKED/PARTIAL status;
- code change/commit and regression results only if tested.

This is an **upstream-source-first, experiment-first** task. The Stack Overflow answer and AOSP source are already cited above, so the coder must not be sent away to perform generic internet research. Their job is to prove/refute the match against MiniAndroid.

## Source status
- External direct answer: FOUND (Stack Overflow accepted answer; published 2022).
- Authoritative upstream mechanism: FOUND (AndroidX Recomposer source).
- Local historical symptom: OBSERVED in CONT-25 / UPP-005.
- Exact local causal match at current HEAD: NOT YET PROVEN.
- Runtime fix by this research pass: NOT IMPLEMENTED / NOT TESTED.

# CONT-81 — Controlled DexSQL value test for MiniAndroid: does it help us fix apps that do not load?

This is a new workstream INSIDE master Issue #364, not a new GitHub issue. The user asks whether DexSQL is genuinely valuable for MiniAndroid—especially APKs that fail to load or show a white screen—and how much it improves engineering throughput. Do not assume it is valuable because it is fast or SQL-based. Run a controlled experiment and make a keep/remove decision based on measured outcomes.

## 0. What DexSQL can and cannot do (do not confuse the layers)
DexSQL is a DEX/APK inspection and query tool: it can query classes, methods, strings, references/callers, instructions/Smali, manifest/components, and resources, and can be exposed to an AI agent through MCP. It is NOT a DEX interpreter, Android framework, renderer, or compatibility fix. It cannot directly make a broken APK run. Its possible value is reducing time to locate the first failing call/contract, identifying repeated roots across many APKs, and producing more precise fixtures/fixes. Runtime success must be measured separately.

Public upstream references to verify against current versions:
- Project/readme and install instructions: https://github.com/LSPosed/dexsql-release
- Docs: https://dexsql.lsposed.org/docs/
- Benchmark methodology/limitations and reproducible data: https://github.com/LSPosed/dexsql-release/tree/master/benchmarks
- Android runtime/DEX semantics background: https://source.android.google.cn/docs/core/runtime?hl=en
- Chinese documentation: https://github.com/LSPosed/dexsql-release/blob/master/README.zh-CN.md
Read the actual license and release checksums before installing; the binary is described as closed-source under DexSQL Binary License 1.0 while the public skill/docs/benchmark materials have different licensing. Do not fetch an arbitrary executable or bypass checksum/license checks. The published benchmark is mostly a query/indexing workload on QQ 9.3.55 and does NOT prove faster MiniAndroid root-cause diagnosis or more apps launching.

## 1. Hypothesis and explicit falsification
Hypothesis: DexSQL + a well-designed query library can speed up source-first DEX investigation for MiniAndroid's real blank/failed-load corpus, especially obfuscated Kotlin/Compose, reflection, missing framework calls, delegate/null crashes, class/method lookup, and call-chain discovery.
Null hypothesis: the existing workflow (current source/trace tools, APK metadata, existing parsers/search) is already fast enough; DexSQL adds setup/query complexity without producing more root fixes or verified app-content wins.

DexSQL is successful only if it improves one or more end-to-end engineering measures while preserving accuracy:
- less wall-clock time to identify the first divergence;
- fewer manual tool/process steps and less context/token use;
- more correctly clustered shared roots across unrelated APKs;
- more generic root fixes accepted by regression tests;
- more APKs reaching verified app-owned content or interaction.
Fast SQL query time alone is not success.

## 2. Corpus: real failed-to-load cases, not a cherry-picked demo
Synchronize to current origin/main and select at least 30 distinct APKs from the existing registry/corpus:
- 20 failed/white/splash/partial cases, spanning at least five failure families (Compose/first frame, WindowRoot/lifecycle, reflection/class resolution, framework API/null receiver, Canvas/Drawable/resources, Kotlin/delegate, native-surface boundary as present);
- 5 known-working controls with similar API families;
- 5 additional unknown/frontier candidates.
Prefer x86/x86_64 artifacts for runtime tests; label ARM-only prerequisites separately. Deduplicate by APK SHA-256 and package; record version, ABI/native libraries, DEX count/size, obfuscation indicators, and current-head verdict. If 30 artifacts are not available, report exact counts and missing artifacts—never invent targets or count retries as new APKs.

## 3. Paired experiment: DexSQL ON versus current baseline
For every selected APK, run two investigation arms against the same binary/runtime revision and same initial failure evidence:
A. Baseline: current MiniAndroid workflow (manifest/resources + existing source/trace/search tools).
B. DexSQL-assisted: the same investigator gets DexSQL CLI/MCP and a reusable query cookbook.
Counterbalance order or split matched cases to reduce learning bias. Keep a time budget per APK and record setup cost separately. Do not let the DexSQL arm see the baseline's final answer. Use a second reviewer or a blinded validation pass where practical.

For each arm record:
- active investigation minutes and elapsed minutes;
- number of tool calls/commands and model tokens if measurable;
- first-divergence stage identified and exact method/class/descriptor;
- evidence chain: failing instruction/callsite → caller chain → expected framework contract → local MiniAndroid implementation → minimal fixture;
- root-cause hypothesis confidence before validation;
- whether the diagnosis survives independent trace/fixture verification;
- root cluster ID and other APKs predicted to share it;
- fix produced, regression outcome, and verified runtime outcome after the fix.
Use identical evidence criteria for both arms. A DexSQL-generated explanation is not evidence until checked against bytecode/source and runtime traces.

## 4. Required DexSQL query cookbook
Create reusable, versioned SQL queries (with saved outputs/hashes) for:
1. Manifest launch activity, exported components, providers, services, permissions and package/version metadata.
2. DEX inventory, class/method counts, class definitions and duplicate/conflicting definitions across multidex.
3. Search exact strings, exception text, framework API names, resource identifiers and class descriptors.
4. Find direct callers/callees and two-hop call chains for a failing method, with full prototypes/descriptors to avoid name-only ambiguity.
5. Locate invoke-* instructions, field accesses, casts, allocations, null-sensitive receiver paths, try/catch ranges, and method instruction windows around the failing PC where supported by DexSQL schema.
6. Reflection paths: Class.forName, getDeclaredMethod/Field, constructor lookup, service/provider/component instantiation, Kotlin reflection and generated delegate access.
7. Compose/Kotlin entry chains: Application → Activity → setContent/ComposeView → content lambda/navigation → measure/layout/draw-related calls, where visible in DEX.
8. Canvas/Bitmap/Path/Drawable/Window/Looper/MessageQueue/Surface/EGL callsites and overloads.
9. Find every APK in the selected corpus referencing the same method/string/descriptor and rank candidate shared roots.
10. Compare working vs failing APKs at equivalent framework API/call-chain boundaries.
11. Identify missing classes and method references, but distinguish genuinely absent definitions from platform-provided APIs, reflection-only references, optional features, and native/JNI methods.
12. Extract resource/manifest evidence adjacent to code references; query packaged layouts/drawables where supported.
13. Save a small set of reproducible queries and machine-readable result digests; never paste entire DEX tables into the model context when a narrow query suffices.

First inspect DexSQL's current schema/docs and supported operations. Do not invent table or column names. If any proposed query cannot be expressed, record it as unsupported and use the baseline tool instead.

## 5. Root-fix loop: DexSQL must help change runtime outcomes
From the 20 failed cases, find repeated first-divergence clusters. Select at least three high-fan-out candidates for source/semantic-law validation. For each:
- compare DexSQL findings with current runtime trace and MiniAndroid source;
- make a minimal positive/negative fixture before editing;
- identify whether the fault is in the app, DEX/library semantics, framework contract, renderer, native/ABI prerequisite, or harness;
- implement only a generic, contract-correct fix when supported;
- retest at least five related corpus APKs, not only the original;
- run three clean executions for claimed wins and prove app-owned content with provenance, screenshot SHA-256/pixel census and (for interactive claims) actual state change.
A query result that merely identifies the crash site, or a patch that only changes the screenshot hash, is not a compatibility win.

## 6. Mandatory performance and accuracy metrics
Report raw values and deltas, not adjectives:
- median and P90 minutes to first correct root cause, A vs B;
- percentage of cases with a correct first-divergence diagnosis;
- false-positive root claims and missed roots;
- shared-root cluster precision (how many grouped cases truly share the same defect);
- number of minimal fixtures created;
- generic fixes accepted and regressions introduced;
- apps newly reaching REAL_APP_CONTENT and INTERACTIVE_CONTENT_VERIFIED;
- setup/installation time, peak RAM, binary size, query latency and model context/tokens if available;
- total cases that were blocked by missing APKs/ABI/harness, excluded from runtime-failure denominators.
Report confidence limits or raw per-case rows; 30 cases is a pilot, not a universal performance guarantee.

## 7. Safety, license, reproducibility and constraints
- Confirm the exact DexSQL release, platform, SHA-256 checksum, license terms and third-party notices. Do not commit the binary, APKs, proprietary decompilation dumps or secrets to the repo.
- Keep all APK analysis local; do not upload APK contents to external services. Check what MCP tool results the client sends to the model.
- No paid API, server, GPU or VPS. Keep RAM use within the project's practical budget; benchmark DexSQL and the baseline on the actual available machine, not the upstream author's hardware.
- Record exact commands, tool versions, environment, queries and output hashes. If DexSQL cannot be installed in the agent environment, run the same cookbook through CLI/web playground only if the artifact and privacy policy allow it, or mark BLOCKED and evaluate a local existing alternative. Do not claim MCP works without an initialize/open/query smoke test.

## 8. Decision gate
Choose one, with evidence:
- **ADOPT**: measurable reduction in diagnosis time (target ≥25% median on the eligible failed corpus) AND no accuracy regression, plus at least two verified shared-root fixes or equivalent increase in correct cross-app triage.
- **LIMITED ADOPT**: useful only for specific tasks (e.g. caller/reference lookup), with measured per-task benefit; publish exactly which workflows should use it.
- **NO MATERIAL BENEFIT**: improvement below threshold or no effect on verified runtime wins; document why and avoid adding workflow overhead.
- **BLOCKED**: setup/license/environment prevents a valid trial; state exact blocker and next smallest experiment.
The 25% target is an evaluation threshold, not a pre-known result.

## 9. Deliverables in this same #364 issue
Commit a concise report, query cookbook, paired per-APK CSV/JSONL results, corpus manifest hash, exact tool/binary hashes (not the binary itself), reproducible runner, and at least one validated example from a genuinely non-loading APK. Post the report here with:
- the result table and raw denominators;
- three strongest DexSQL discoveries and whether each led to a generic fix;
- apps whose runtime result actually changed (before/after);
- all cases where DexSQL was slower/wrong/unhelpful;
- adoption decision and next action.
Keep all existing runtime regression gates green. Do not mark #364 complete just because DexSQL is installed or the report is polished.

# CONT-92 — TELEGRAM SOURCE-GROUNDED COMPLETION ADDENDUM (official upstream code → MiniAndroid contracts → real screens)

This supplements CONT-88/89; it does not replace them and does not create a new Issue. I inspected the public upstream source entry points:
- Official-source mirror: https://github.com/TelegramOrg/Telegram-Android
- Upstream canonical repository: https://github.com/DrKLO/Telegram
- LaunchActivity.java (very large activity/navigation entry point): https://github.com/DrKLO/Telegram/blob/master/TMessagesProj/src/main/java/org/telegram/ui/LaunchActivity.java
- LoginActivity.java: https://github.com/DrKLO/Telegram/blob/master/TMessagesProj/src/main/java/org/telegram/ui/LoginActivity.java
- DialogsActivity.java: https://github.com/TelegramOrg/Telegram-Android/blob/master/TMessagesProj/src/main/java/org/telegram/ui/DialogsActivity.java
- ChatActivity.java: https://github.com/DrKLO/Telegram/blob/master/TMessagesProj/src/main/java/org/telegram/ui/ChatActivity.java
- RecyclerListView.java: https://github.com/DrKLO/Telegram/blob/master/TMessagesProj/src/main/java/org/telegram/ui/Components/RecyclerListView.java
- BaseFragment.java: https://github.com/DrKLO/Telegram/blob/master/TMessagesProj/src/main/java/org/telegram/ui/ActionBar/BaseFragment.java
- Main manifest: https://github.com/TelegramOrg/Telegram-Android/blob/master/TMessagesProj/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml

Source inspection establishes that Telegram is not a simple XML-screen app: core screens are built through large Java classes, custom fragment/navigation abstractions, RecyclerView-derived RecyclerListView, custom ActionBar/Theme/Drawable/view components, runtime resources, and native/build submodules. For example LaunchActivity is ~9k lines, LoginActivity ~10k lines, DialogsActivity ~14k lines, RecyclerListView ~3.9k lines and ChatActivity is multi-megabyte. This makes a single “Telegram UI fix” or copying one activity the wrong approach. The goal is to identify missing Android semantic contracts in MiniAndroid that prevent these upstream call paths from completing.

IMPORTANT honesty: source browsing is not a complete source audit and not proof the current MiniAndroid APK is built from the exact same Telegram commit. First pin the APK SHA-256, manifest/version, package, ABI and source revision. Then correlate the APK's methods/resources with this source version. If exact version/source match cannot be established, label the source as a close upstream reference, not exact source-of-truth.

## Deliverable A — exact source-to-runtime dependency map
Create a compact, machine-readable ledger for each target screen:
- Source method/call site and source commit/hash;
- corresponding DEX class/method/signature in the exact APK;
- framework/library API it invokes;
- MiniAndroid implementation file/dispatch path;
- runtime event/result/exception and state before/after;
- expected next UI transition;
- evidence for first divergence and root ID (or explicitly NOT_YET_TRACED).
Start with LaunchActivity startup/fragment host, LoginActivity login stages, DialogsActivity list, ChatActivity conversation/composer, BaseFragment lifecycle/navigation, RecyclerListView adapter/recycling, ActionBar/Theme, AndroidUtilities density/insets, image/resource loaders and relevant native JNI calls. Do not copy Telegram UI implementation into MiniAndroid.

## Deliverable B — a bounded but real end-to-end journey
Attempt these in order on current HEAD, using real touch/key input:
1. cold start → actual login/phone screen;
2. focus and type into phone field;
3. activate Next and record exact reachable result;
4. code/SMS stage only if safely deterministic/reachable; otherwise record the actual network/auth boundary;
5. post-login Dialogs only with genuine available session/data or a clearly isolated deterministic test fixture;
6. search/filter transition;
7. open an actual available conversation, verify ChatActivity root, list/empty state and composer;
8. Back returns to correct previous state;
9. reachable settings/profile screen and Back restoration.
Do not fake authentication, synthesize private chats, or label a fixture as real account data. When remote auth/data is unavailable, complete every offline/reachable screen and distinguish service prerequisite from runtime defect.

## Deliverable C — instrument the whole visual pipeline, not just ViewTree or draw-op counts
For each stage log a bounded event with monotonic timestamp, thread, activity/fragment/root identity, dimensions and causal ID:
1. Activity lifecycle and Fragment transaction queued/committed/executed;
2. decor/window/root attach and visibility;
3. resource/theme/configuration resolution;
4. ViewGroup add/remove, LayoutParams class and resolved margins/gravity;
5. measure specs/results, layout bounds, scroll offsets, clipping and insets;
6. RecyclerView adapter count/view type/create/bind/attach/recycle, stable ID if used, layout manager and child bounds;
7. TextView/StaticLayout/Spannable/font metrics and Drawable/Bitmap resolved type/bounds;
8. onDraw/dispatchDraw and Canvas op source;
9. RenderNode recording/replay and raster target;
10. selected final window/surface and present;
11. touch/key dispatch, focused view, listener/DEX target and state delta.
Every event must correlate across phases; cap logs and avoid recording secrets, phone numbers, message contents or auth tokens. Use structural fingerprints rather than sensitive text.

## Deliverable D — prioritized failure hypotheses with decisive tests
These are hypotheses to test, not assumed causes:
- Fragment root is created but attached to a stale/wrong container, or transaction timing differs;
- custom LayoutParams/MeasureSpec/gravity/insets resolves critical views to zero size/out of viewport;
- RecyclerListView adapter count is zero because data/controller callback did not run, versus count>0 but rows were not created/bound/attached;
- child rows exist but custom onDraw, clipping, alpha, canvas transforms or render-node replay loses pixels;
- Theme.ResourcesProvider, resource ID, density or stateful Drawable resolves incorrectly;
- StaticLayout/Spannable/typeface fallback or custom text measurement produces wrong/zero bounds;
- custom ActionBar/overlay/window ordering obscures the correct content;
- app initialization is stuck behind SQLite, native library/JNI, coroutine/executor, notification/permission or remote auth/network boundary;
- frame budget/slow interpreter prevents first present even though a later trace shows draw operations.
For each hypothesis, specify one minimal fixture or instrumentation probe that falsifies it. Never patch a hypothesis without an observed first divergence.

## Deliverable E — root fixes that transfer to other apps
For each confirmed root:
1. reproduce on Telegram or a minimal fixture;
2. reproduce the same contract violation in at least one unrelated app (two independent apps preferred);
3. fix generic MiniAndroid/AOSP behavior, not org.telegram.* branches;
4. add positive + negative fixture tests;
5. run Telegram, Forkgram and at least three unrelated View/RecyclerView apps where APKs exist;
6. rerun all gates and compare before/after screenshot hashes/pixel metrics and state.
Update #363's existing roadmap and root registry; do not create a duplicate goal/root. A diagnostic improvement alone is not a compatibility fix.

## Deliverable F — screenshot and state acceptance
For each screen claimed:
- 3 cold/warm controlled runs (record which);
- exact APK SHA-256, source revision, runtime commit, binary SHA-256;
- screenshot SHA-256, non-background/unique-color/edge metrics, root/window ID and app-owned pixel provenance;
- stable expected UI landmarks/bounds (not exact-pixel-only if rendering variation is legitimate);
- trace and first-divergence pointer;
- input sequence, resulting state delta and visible result;
- regression result.
Classify separately: SOURCE_INSPECTED, DEX_PATH_TRACED, LIFECYCLE_REACHED, VIEWTREE_PRESENT, MEASURED, DRAW_OPS_PRESENT, PIXELS_PRESENT, REAL_APP_CONTENT, INTERACTIVE. Never jump from one class to a later one based on an earlier stage.

## Delivery F — specific shared UI contracts to audit from the upstream code
Prioritize:
1. custom fragment attach/detach/back-stack and transaction execution;
2. Activity window/decor/root ownership, dialogs/sheets/overlays and insets;
3. custom LayoutHelper/LayoutParams mapping, dp/density, gravity, measure and clip;
4. RecyclerListView/RecyclerView layout manager, adapter lifecycle, item recycling, scroll and empty view;
5. custom ActionBar/Theme.ResourcesProvider and stateful Drawable/color filters;
6. Canvas transforms/clips/alpha/text/image drawing and RenderNode begin/record/replay;
7. text shaping: Spannable spans, StaticLayout, baseline/line height, bidi, emoji and typeface fallback;
8. image decode/bounds/density/rounded clipping and async image completion;
9. main-thread dispatch, Handler/Looper/choreographer/frame invalidation and bounded async work;
10. DEX reflection, class initialization and exception propagation encountered by actual call sites;
